Letting $x=-5$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{(n+5)(-5)^n}{n^2\cdot5^n}
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{(n+5)(-1)^n5^n}{n^2\cdot5^n}\\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }(-1)^n\cdot \dfrac{(n+5)}{n^2}
\end{align}$

By the alternating series test, we know this series converges.

Letting $x=5$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+5)(5)^n}{n^2\cdot5^n} 
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+5)}{n^2}\\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{1}{n} + \dfrac{5}{n^2}
\end{align}$

By direct comparison with the harmonic series, we know this series diverges.
